00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
GETTING SUED:
By now, I’m sure you’ve heard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.560
Amazon getting sued. The FTC and 17 states have&nbsp;
sued Amazon for maintaining an illegal monopoly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.960
within the e-commerce market. The outcome of this&nbsp;
case could very well be Amazon getting split up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:20.960
into not just 2 companies but several companies.&nbsp;
Also, it’s not just Amazon that would be affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.360
as this case would very much set a precedent for&nbsp;
all of big tech in general, potentially leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:32.800
to even more breakups and landmark cases. The idea&nbsp;
of Amazon finally being held accountable has made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.280
much of the internet quite happy because let’s&nbsp;
be honest, Amazon is definitely a monopoly. Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:46.560
year, nearly 60% of all online retail purchases&nbsp;
were made on Amazon. If that’s not a monopoly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:53.240
I don’t know what is. But, this doesn’t have to be&nbsp;
bad news for Amazon. In fact, this lawsuit could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.280
turn into the best thing that ever happened to&nbsp;
Amazon. Here’s the thing, whether you or me or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.000
entire world believes that Amazon is a monopoly is&nbsp;
for the most part completely irrelevant. All that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.880
matters is whether that argument actually holds&nbsp;
up within the court of law. And let me just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.680
Amazon has a far better chance at court than they&nbsp;
do with public opinion. As Amazon put it while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.560
it’s true that most online retail purchases come&nbsp;
through them, they don't exactly end up with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.720
In fact, Amazon claims that they’re supporting&nbsp;
500,000 independent businesses and 1.5 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.280
jobs within the US alone. With that being said,&nbsp;
Amazon would’ve definitely preferred for this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.920
to never come up in the first place but at the&nbsp;
same time they likely knew that it was inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.800
and they can very much use it in their favor.&nbsp;
Here’s the thing, if Amazon is able to come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.400
on top with this case, no prosecutor would likely&nbsp;
touch them for the next 20-30 years. In other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.480
words, winning this case would basically give them&nbsp;
a free pass to keep doing exactly what they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.920
doing with little fear of legal repercussions.&nbsp;
Don’t believe me? Well join me as we take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.146
look back at a few landmark cases that show that&nbsp;
this case could just as easily go Amazon’s way.

00:02:07.146 --> 00:02:15.640
MICROSOFT GOT AWAY:
Speaking of monopolies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.800
if you’d like to help us take on the big bank&nbsp;
monopoly and earn 4-7% yields in the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.760
check out our upcoming bond investing app&nbsp;
Silo in the description below. But anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.280
getting straight into it, if you’ve been watching&nbsp;
this channel for a while, you probably alread know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:36.200
what case I’m gonna talk about. It’s none other&nbsp;
than the landmark case United States vs Microsoft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.280
If you’re not familiar with this case, this&nbsp;
was probably before your time but essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.360
this case was a daring bid to break up Microsoft&nbsp;
into 2 companies: one that sold operating systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.960
and one that sold other pieces of software&nbsp;
like Internet Explorer, Microsoft Office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:58.440
MSN Messenger and so on. The roots of this case&nbsp;
date back to mid 1990s when the internet was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:04.360
starting to become a thing. The go to browser&nbsp;
for 90% of internet users was something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:11.520
Netscape Navigator. So, they were very much a&nbsp;
monopoly which Microsoft wasn’t exactly a fan of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.840
You may not have known this but these companies&nbsp;
only like it when they’re the monopoly. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:23.080
on August 16, 1995, Microsoft decided to take&nbsp;
things into their own hands by launching Internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:29.320
Explorer. Given Microsoft’s resources, putting&nbsp;
together a functional browser wasn’t particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:34.880
hard but getting people to use it, man, that&nbsp;
was difficult. But fortunately, Microsoft had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.440
a sneaky plan to get around this little conundrum.&nbsp;
Everyone already had Windows computers, so what if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.280
Microsoft just bundled in Internet Explorer with&nbsp;
Windows? That should give IE a massive boost in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:53.280
popularity straight out the gate right? Well,&nbsp;
there was just one fatal flaw with this plan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.840
it was illegal. You see, the DOJ had already seen&nbsp;
this coming. They saw Microsoft do it with Office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.760
and they had since put a deal in place that&nbsp;
would prevent Microsoft from ever doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.560
again. There was one caveat to this deal though.&nbsp;
Microsoft wasn’t allowed to bundle in products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:17.000
with Windows but they were allowed to bundle in&nbsp;
features. So, they would just call IE feature and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.440
bundle it in with Windows. Obviously, everyone in&nbsp;
the entire world knows that IE is a product and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.080
that this is in violation of their previous DOJ&nbsp;
settlement. But, Microsoft probably just thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:34.720
we can roll the dice with an anti trust lawsuit&nbsp;
if it ever came to it. And guess what, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:42.800
come to it. On May 18, 1998, the DOJ and 20 states&nbsp;
would file an antitrust lawsuit against Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.400
just like what we’re seeing right now with Amazon.&nbsp;
The case would drag on for about 2 years and both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.280
Microsoft and the DOJ would have their own share&nbsp;
of victories and losses but at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.280 --> 00:05:01.360
the DOJ would prevail. On June 7, 2000, Microsoft&nbsp;
would officially be ordered to split up into 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:08.240
different companies. Most people thought that this&nbsp;
was the end of it. Microsoft was a monopoly. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.440
knew it, the DOJ knew, the whole world knew it,&nbsp;
and now it was made official and it was time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:20.240
Microsoft to face the music. Unfortunately though,&nbsp;
it didn’t end up playing out like this. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.240
Microsoft would appeal to the Supreme Court and&nbsp;
they would end up overturning this decision over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.800
a legal technicality. You see, the judge that was&nbsp;
overseeing the case had regularly made statements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.200
on the media because the public wanted to stay&nbsp;
up to date. And Microsoft would end up arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.760
that his statements to the media suggested that&nbsp;
he was biased against Microsoft. The Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.680
Court would rule that the judge’s findings and&nbsp;
conclusions were all well and good. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:55.080
due to apparent bias, his final ruling was not.&nbsp;
As such, Microsoft would be allowed to settle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:01.240
Let me put this another way. The DOJ literally&nbsp;
proved that Microsoft was a monopoly in court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:06.440
and Microsoft still wasn’t broken up because of&nbsp;
legal technicality that likely not even Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:12.600
cared about. As far as they were concerned, this&nbsp;
was just a loophole to get out the situation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:19.320
it worked. Fast forward 23 years and not only&nbsp;
is Microsoft 10 times bigger but they’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:25.640
bundling in Edge with Windows. So, just because a&nbsp;
massive case is filed against Amazon doesn’t mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:30.960
anything. As far as we know, a judge could point&nbsp;
blank rule for Amazon to be broken up and it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:37.440
still go the other way. Until they’re actually&nbsp;
broken up, they’re not. But, even on the off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:51.375
chance that they do get broken, it probably&nbsp;
wont matter all that much. Let me explain.

00:06:51.375 --> 00:06:52.800
BREAK UPS DON’T MATTER:
A lot of people seem to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.000
that breaking up a monopoly such as Amazon is&nbsp;
exactly the solution we need. But, in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:05.160
this isn’t all that effective; in fact, I would&nbsp;
argue that it’s for the most part ineffective and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.320
we’ve seen this repeatedly throughout history,&nbsp;
starting with Standard Oil. Standard Oil was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.720
brainchild of oil pioneer John D Rockefeller&nbsp;
and it was definitely a monopoly. Going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:24.080
the 1900s, Standard oil controlled 91% of oil&nbsp;
production and 85% of final sales in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:31.280
States. So, in 1906, Uncle Sam decided to step&nbsp;
in and teach Standard Oil a lesson. The case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.360
would drag on for 5 years but eventually&nbsp;
prosecutors would get their way. In 1911,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.880
Standard Oil would be split up into not just 1 or&nbsp;
2 or even 5 companies. They would be split up into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:50.000
34 separate entities. Surely, such drastic action&nbsp;
would neutralize the company because they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.640
have to compete against each other right? Well,&nbsp;
it actually did the exact opposite. You see, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.920
leaders of these companies may have had internal&nbsp;
conflicts amongst themselves before the split up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.400
but after the split up, they were more united&nbsp;
than ever. The situation had changed from you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:14.680
vs me to us vs the world. This was also around the&nbsp;
time that the Ford Model T would go viral meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.400
that more people needed oil than ever before.&nbsp;
And instead of fighting over these customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:24.440
Standard Oil companies would unite and dominate&nbsp;
and the market together. Investors would do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:29.240
exact same thing. Now that the antitrust&nbsp;
cloud wasn’t looming over Standard Oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.680
investors would be far more willing to&nbsp;
invest. And if you don’t believe me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.520
just look at the facts. In the 10 years that&nbsp;
followed the break up, the combined value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:45.520
the 34 companies didn’t just grow, it quintupled.&nbsp;
This took Rockefeller from being a mere mortal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:52.800
worth $300 million to being the world’s first&nbsp;
billionaire. The irony of that is insane. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.080
case that was supposed to put a decisive end&nbsp;
to people like Rockefeller literally ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:03.600
up creating the world’s first billionaire. And it&nbsp;
doesn’t just stop right there either. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.880
the strongest of these companies would make their&nbsp;
partnerships official and merge to create Exxon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:15.400
Chevron, and BP. Over a 100 years after the&nbsp;
break up, these 3 are all still part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:22.080
top 10 oil companies with a combined market value&nbsp;
of just under a trillion dollars. You might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.680
inclined to say that this was just a fluke and&nbsp;
that Standard Oil just got lucky which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:35.400
us into our next landmark lawsuit: United States&nbsp;
vs AT&amp;T. Back in the 1970s, AT&amp;T found themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:41.120
controlling 90% of the US telephone business.&nbsp;
Seeing this, Uncle Sam decided that it was time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:47.880
to put an end to this disgusting monopoly. After&nbsp;
an 8 year lawsuit, in 1982, AT&amp;T would agree to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:53.520
split up their business into 7 smaller regional&nbsp;
businesses called “Baby Bells”. Surely, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.240
would pit these companies against each other and&nbsp;
lead to a competitive landscape. But, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:05.520
the exact opposite happened. The Baby Bells would&nbsp;
end up joining forces in record time. Here’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:11.920
flowchart of exactly what happened. The Baby Bells&nbsp;
would basically just unite to create AT&amp;T and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:17.960
Verizon, the two players who still dominate the US&nbsp;
telecommunications industry to this day. I do want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:23.680
to note that T Mobile was able make a name for&nbsp;
themselves during this time period but it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.040
because of the breaup. It was because there was&nbsp;
a new market: wireless telecommunications which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:34.080
allowed for new competition to capitlize on. Not&nbsp;
to mention, T Mobile was backed by the German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.960
telecommunications monopoly: Deutsche Telekom. So,&nbsp;
it wasn’t really a case of an underdog taking on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:45.760
Verizon and AT&amp;T. It was actually just a case of&nbsp;
an international monopoly taking on 2 national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:51.120
monopolies and together, they still control&nbsp;
the entire market. So, who knows? Breaking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.040
Amazon may actually play out better for Amazon&nbsp;
over the long term. Instead of being worth a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:02.680
mere $150 billion, Jeff Bezos could become the&nbsp;
world’s first trillionaire. Jokes aside though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:15.379
just like an antitrust lawsuit isn’t necessarily&nbsp;
a nail in the coffin, a break up isn’t either.

